# Registry of tracked files that may name withheld material, and of tracked
# files this scanner cannot read.
#
# Checked by: ./script/check-internal-refs.sh
# Rule:       a file in a public repository does not point at material that is
#             deliberately not in it — see script/internal-refs.deny.
#
# This is NOT a place to silence findings. Every entry must carry a line
# starting literally with
#
#     because: ...
#
# and every `file ::` entry must carry
#
#     occurrences: <number>
#
# Without either one the gate calls this file damaged and exits 2.
#
# ── WHY THE COUNT IS PINNED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# An exception without a number covers not only the reference that was read and
# accepted, but every reference the file will ever grow. That is how an
# exception turns into a hiding place: the one file allowed to name the
# withheld directories becomes the one file where a new dead link can be added
# in silence.
#
# So the number is part of the entry. A new reference in an allowed file
# changes the count and the gate goes red on it, naming the file — which is the
# point: somebody has to read the new line and raise the number on purpose.
#
# A count that falls to zero is red as well. An exception that is no longer
# needed is a stale claim about the code, and this repository has paid for
# stale claims more than once.

# ── files that legitimately name the withheld directories ────────────────────

file :: .gitignore
  occurrences: 3
  because: this is the file that DOES the excluding. Two of the three names
  because: here are entries that keep those directories out of the repository,
  because: and the third is the throwaway-worktree entry. A rule cannot be
  because: written without naming what it applies to, and there is nothing dead
  because: about these: they are the reason the paths resolve to nothing.
  because:
  because: The count moving is worth a look rather than a shrug: a new line
  because: here that names withheld material is usually a NEW withheld
  because: directory, and that is exactly the moment it also has to be added
  because: to script/internal-refs.deny — which nothing else would remind
  because: anybody to do.

file :: script/textgrep.py
  occurrences: 1
  because: a scanner, told not to descend into the agent's own notes. Same
  because: shape as the line above: naming a directory in order to skip it is
  because: not a pointer to a document inside it. Left un-named, the scanner
  because: would walk into thousands of untracked files on the owner's machine
  because: and answer questions about material nobody asked about.

file :: script/internal-refs.deny
  occurrences: 5
  because: the list of forbidden paths, which cannot exist without spelling
  because: them out. It names the directories and nothing inside them — no
  because: document, no date, no section — and the reader who follows it finds
  because: the explanation of why the path is absent, which is the opposite of
  because: a dead link.

file :: script/check_internal_refs.py
  occurrences: 2
  because: both are inside worked examples in comments, showing where the
  because: match ends: the directory name matches, the same name with letters
  because: glued onto it does not. Concrete beats abstract for the person who
  because: has to change this matcher, and the examples name a directory, not
  because: anything in it.

# ── files this scanner cannot read, and what they are ────────────────────────
#
# Not skipping them quietly is deliberate. The one thing worse than a scanner
# that misses a file is a scanner that misses it in silence and reports a clean
# tree — which is not hypothetical here. The search wrapper the shells in this
# project get runs with `-I`, so a file carrying a raw NUL is skipped without a
# word: measured on a file whose text plainly contains a withheld path, the
# wrapper answers "no match" and /usr/bin/grep finds it. That silence has left
# "single source" checks green forever before.
#
# Every file listed here has been opened and confirmed to be what it claims to
# be. If one of them ever starts decoding as text, the gate goes red and asks
# for a second look, because a picture that turned into text is not a picture.

unscannable :: docs/audits/2026-07-25-slither/raw/slither.json.gz
  because: gzip of the raw Slither JSON from the 25 July 2026 audit, kept
  because: compressed because the uncompressed report is megabytes of machine
  because: output nobody reads end to end.

unscannable :: frontend/public/icon-192.png
  because: PWA icon, 192px. A PNG.

unscannable :: frontend/public/icon-512.png
  because: PWA icon, 512px. A PNG.

unscannable :: frontend/src/app/favicon.ico
  because: the browser tab icon, in the ICO container format.

unscannable :: frontend/src/app/icon.png
  because: the app icon Next.js serves from the app directory. A PNG.
